# Byte-level reproducibility is a claim this repository makes, so line-ending
# normalisation is disabled wherever it would break that claim.
#
# The observation writer pins LF explicitly (ObsWriter opens with newline="\n")
# precisely so the same run produces the same bytes on Windows and Linux. Git's
# default on Windows (core.autocrlf=true) would convert those files on add and
# checkout, and a clone would then hash differently from the archive the results
# were measured on. `-text` tells git to treat a file as binary and never touch it.

*.jsonl   -text
*.json    -text
*.sha256  -text

# This repository's own source and prose: normalise to LF, so a Windows clone and a
# Linux clone compile and hash identically.
*.py      text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf

# PowerShell is the exception: Windows tooling expects CRLF.
*.ps1     text eol=crlf

# Extensionless files match none of the rules above, so they fall through to
# core.autocrlf -- which on Windows rewrites them in the working tree and leaves
# them permanently "modified" against the index. Pin them explicitly.
LICENSE        text eol=lf

# Git's own control files: keep LF so `git add` stops warning about them.
.gitattributes text eol=lf
.gitignore     text eol=lf

# LAST RULE WINS, and this one has to be last.
#
# experiments/ is the vendored harness tree: a set of hash-pinned artifacts, not
# source this repository edits. Thirteen of its files carry CRLF endings upstream,
# and the digests in VENDOR_PINS.json were computed over those exact bytes. The
# `*.py text eol=lf` rule above would rewrite them on the first `git add` and
# verify_vendor.py would fail on the first commit — reporting drift that git itself
# had introduced. Vendored, pinned files must pass through untouched.
experiments/** -text
